<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apennines</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Apennine Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Apennines are the mountain range that runs about 1,200 kilometres down the length of the Italian peninsula, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. Their valleys and uplands were home to many ancient Italic peoples, such as the Samnites, Sabines and Umbrians, and their passes linked the Tyrrhenian and Adriatic sides of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The highest peak is Corno Grande in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-sasso">Gran Sasso</a> massif.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apennines.jpg" alt="Apennines" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luciodem · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Il_Gran_Sasso_d%27Italia,_il_paretone_nord.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Genoa was an old Ligurian port, squeezed between the <b>Apennines</b> and the sea, which had suffered Muslim raids in the tenth century and by the eleventh was raiding back, joining Pisa in attacks on Sardinia and on Mahdia in 1087.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake originating in the Central <b>Apennines</b> was felt in Rome and Spoleto and damaged the basilica of San Paolo Fuori le Mura.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-29">29 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Agrippa Menenius Lanatus, a consul of the Roman Republic, celebrated a triumph for his victory over the Sabines, an Italic people of the central <b>Apennine Mountains</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aequi lived in the upper valleys of the Aniene, Turano and Salto rivers in the <b>Apennines</b> east of Latium.</blockquote><a href="/state/aequi">Aequi</a></li><li><blockquote>It controlled the Via Emilia and a route across the <b>Apennines</b> to Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-parma">Commune of Parma</a></li><li><blockquote>Pistoia, at the foot of the <b>Apennines</b> between Florence and Lucca, had consuls by 1105.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-pistoia">Commune of Pistoia</a></li></ul></section>
